045585] [17592186407878 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407876 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407878 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129340" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407878 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407878 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine ${lim}_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{3}-xy}{{x}^{3}y}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407878 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "113d22b7-0222-5499-8d44-de1af29900a0" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407878 #edb/ref 1074 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{3}-xy}{{x}^{3}y} & =\\frac{{0}^{3}-2(0)}{{2}^{3}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{3}-xy}{{x}^{3}y} & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{y({y}^{2}-x)}{{x}^{3}y} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{\\cancel{y}({y}^{2}-x)}{{x}^{3}\\cancel{y}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{{0}^{2}-2}{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-\\frac{1}{4}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407879 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407874 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407879 #edb/ref 1069 "q-36139" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407879 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407879 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y}\n$$\n\n- The limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{3}$ along the path $y={x}^{2}$\n- The limit evaluates to $-1$ along the path $y=-{x}^{2}$\n- The limit exists and is equal to $\\frac{1}{3}$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407879 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a9a907de-0404-5ffb-8362-e9676298070b" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407879 #edb/ref 1074 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y}=\\frac{0}{{0}^{2}+2\\cdot 0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement I is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-{x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-{x}^{2}}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2(-{x}^{2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y}=\\text{DNE}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407880 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407875 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407880 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74955" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407880 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407880 #edb/ref 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{xy+2}-2}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407880 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d70f2b2e-153e-52a8-b763-677074637b88" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407880 #edb/ref 1074 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{xy+2}-2}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 1+2}-2}{{2}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}-4}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{xy+2}-2}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{xy+2}-2)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)}{({x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{xy+2})^{2}-4}{({x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{xy-2}{(xy-2)(xy+2)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{1}{(xy+2)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{1}{(xy+2)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)} & =\\frac{1}{(2\\cdot 1+2)(\\sqrt{2\\cdot 1+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{16}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407881 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407877 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407881 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129343" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407881 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407881 #edb/ref 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{y+z}{\\sqrt{x-y}-\\sqrt{x+z}}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407881 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fac05de1-cec1-5741-a5ec-beb8567ada24" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407881 #edb/ref 1074 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{y+z}{\\sqrt{x-y}-\\sqrt{x+z}} & =\\frac{1+(-1)}{\\sqrt{2-1}-\\sqrt{2+(-1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{y+z}{\\sqrt{x-y}-\\sqrt{x+z}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{(y+z)(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})}{(\\sqrt{x-y}-\\sqrt{x+z})(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{(y+z)(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})}{(x-y)-(x+z)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{(y+z)(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})}{-(y+z)} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{\\cancel{(y+z)}(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})}{\\cancel{(y+z)}} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z}) & =-(\\sqrt{2-1}+\\sqrt{2+(-1)}) \\\\  & =-(1+1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407882 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186407885 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407882 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "23171e8d-210d-5c2c-9bfd-bb23fda5150e" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407882 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186377714 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407883 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186407882 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407883 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "61b825c0-1272-5e4c-b3ff-4f0f75ec0886" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407883 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186407880 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407884 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186407883 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407884 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "80843e02-eff6-534a-9c80-13ab1c07a9fb" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407884 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186407879 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407885 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186407886 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407885 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "dd8e3137-e924-50eb-b2e3-83c1445d70ab" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407885 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186407878 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407886 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e8e33d31-3246-51ca-a270-794ee881c78b" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407886 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186407881 #edb/ref 175921860455